# Error messages of the Coordinator and of the manager

This file collects the problems of the user facing error messages of the Coordinator and of the manager (Controllers), and the rules for rewriting them.
The messages are in:
- `Coordinator/src/userMessages/Report.java` (what the user gave us and can change)
- `Coordinator/src/userMessages/Violation.java` (Fearless can no longer do its safe job)
- `Controllers/src/managerMessages/Report.java`
- `Controllers/src/managerMessages/Violation.java`

Agents write bad error messages.
An agent finding a bad message adds it to the list below; it does not reword messages on its own.
The user rewrites them, or guides the rewriting line by line.

## Rules

- A message states what Fearless found and which rule it broke.
- A message does not tell the user what to do to fix the problem: the user may not want to fix it.
- A message does not speak as if Fearless knew what the user wants to do, for example as if the user used git or zip right now.
- Naming a tool that exists, like git or zip, is not an assumption about the user.
  Fearless refuses files that would stop such tools from working, whether or not the user uses them, so that a project keeps working with them later.
- A vague reason is fine when a precise one would go out of date: "Some systems/tools trim these, which causes collisions" stays true while tools come and go.
- A message explains the terms it uses: "This feature is called file locking." is good.
- A technical term is fine in a message only a technical action can reach, provided the message explains the term: "headless" can only come from starting Fearless headless.
- Every cause a message states is a real one, never a guess dressed as a fact.
- A message considers the most likely cause for the target user, a student who starts Fearless from its icon, not a programmer working on the command line.
- A message asking to report a problem names where: https://github.com/FearlessLang/Controllers/issues

The rules text appended to every project scan error (`rulesWall` in `Coordinator/src/userMessages/Report.java`) is the text of the user, and the reference for these messages.
The "How to fix" section that `Report.fail` adds to every project scan error is not: it tells the user what to do, against the rules above.

## Decided

- `Report.rootNotDirectory`: the rules text does not belong to this message: "the project root is not a folder" says nothing about names.
- `Report.symlinkForbidden`: "copy it into the repository" speaks of a repository the user may not have; "Replace the symbolic link with a real file/folder" tells the user what to do, and in established terminology a symbolic link is a real file too. "Symbolic links behave differently across systems and tools, so we forbid them" is fine.
- `Report.onlyRegularFilesAndDirs` and `Report.invisibleOnlyRegularFilesAndDirs`: "Remove it from the project folder. Keep only normal files and folders here" tells the user what to do.
- `Violation.freshCopyThenReport`: "If this keeps happening, report the problem" names no place: the place is https://github.com/FearlessLang/Controllers/issues
- `Violation.mustUseLauncher`: "Do not start fearless directly from the Jars." takes the cause for a programmer running the jars with Java on purpose. The target user never does that: the likely cause is something changing the files of the Fearless program folder, an attack on the machine.
- `Violation.desktopUnsupported`: "headless" stays, and the message explains what headless means.
- `managerMessages.Violation.desktopHidesWindow`: "every program with decorated windows is affected, not only Fearless: on GNOME it means the process that decorates windows (mutter-x11-frames) has died" is a guess, not a known cause.
- `managerMessages.Violation.couldNotUseInstanceLock`: "This feature is called file locking." is good.

## Listed, not yet discussed

- `Report.needsExtension`: "Files normally must be named like "name.ext"" ("normally must"); "Rename it to a well-known extensionless file (example: "readme")" tells the user to rename their file to readme.
- `Report.pathTooLong`: the length is not shown; "move this content closer to the project root".
- `Report.missingExtension`: "change "foo." to "foo.txt"" invents a target name.
- `Report.multiDotExtNotAllowed`: "more than one dot in the extension part"; "Most files"; "OR".
- `Report.extInvalidChar`: the example ".tar.gz" next to the single extension rule.
- `Report.extensionlessMaskExtension`, `Report.invisibleSiblingNamesCollide`, `Report.autoloadedNamesCollide`: "Rename one of them so they are clearly distinct", "Avoid ...".
- `Report.generatedTestNameReserved`: "is almost certainly an accident".
- `Report.zipNoEntries`: "This is most likely a mistake."
- `Report.emptyDirectory`, `Report.zipEmptyDirectoryEntry`: "they may not be supported by compression tools (zip)"; zip stores empty folders, and the second message is about an entry already in a zip.
- `Report.zipNestingTooDeep`: "if recursively unzipped, it would clearly go over the OS path length limit".
- `Report.zipDuplicateEntryName`: "(Schizophrenic ZIP file)"; "different content is seen in different moments".
- `Report.dateInTheFuture`: "This usually comes from ..." lists likely causes, then "Save the file again".
- `Report.zipBadEntryName`: lists every invalid pattern instead of the one the entry breaks.
- `Report.invisibleInvalidSurrogate`: "contains invalid Unicode" does not show the character.
- `Violation.badLaunchArg`: "a broken path" does not say what is broken.
- `managerMessages.Violation.managerFolderIntro`: the manager process and the user processes, explained in about six messages.
- `managerMessages.Violation.blockingPrograms` and "Common fixes are ..." in `couldNotUseInstanceLock`: guessed programs and fixes.
- `managerMessages.Violation.noSystemTray`, `trayIconRemoved`, `trayConnectionLost`: "Restore the system tray of this desktop".
- `managerMessages.Violation.newFilesNotReported`: "Put this Fearless folder on a disk of this computer."
- `managerMessages.Report.registerRefused`, `folderUnreadable`, `claimsNotSaved`: "Give Fearless access to the folder"; `folderMissing`, `markerMissing`: "Restore it, or forget this project".
